title: JushiD2_FoShou
position: 1536,687
---
Jushi: Oh, you even have a Buddha's Hand. That little lid knob is oddly cute.
(Hou presses her lips together.)
I: Buddha's hand is a kind of citrus fruit, shaped like outstretched fingers.
I: Traditionally it's auspicious because "Buddha" and "good fortune" sound alike in Chinese.
I: It's made from the same clay as the [b][i]Longevity Peach Teapot[/i][/b]. Both add decorations to an established teapot form.
(As you speak, you take out two lenticular cards and hand them to Hou.
While she compares and handles them, 
you turn toward the display cabinet, scanning the shelves for the actual teapots.)
//这里暂停一下沟通页面，空格后重新pull up
<<pause_dialogue>>
(Found it! You place the teapot before Hou.)
I: But this one's an overhead-handle teapot, not a basic round design.
(Hou runs her fingers over the teapot.)
I: It is quite different from the Gongchun you bought last time.
Jushi: Good fortune, good karma, lovely meaning.
(She presses her lips together.) 
Jushi: Truth is, a person's good fortune isn't just about this one lifetime.
I: Hmm?
Jushi: You see, we're choosing a teapot because we've developed the taste for it, 
and because we have enough of... [b]this[/b]—
(She gestures as if counting money, then smiles gently.)
Jushi: Once sages reach that level, they can choose where they come into the world.
Jushi:  Really. If you can attain the Way, you can decide where you'll be reborn, and who your parents will be.
Jushi: People! They always choose the mother's egg to enter life through.
Jushi: Entering through the father's sperm is far too unreliable.
Jushi: A man releases tens of thousands of sperm at once. 
Jushi: For just one to join the single egg, the odds are terribly slim.
Jushi: And so many are aborted before they even take shape. 
Jushi: Plus, a woman only has a fixed number of eggs in her whole life. 
Jushi: Being born into a human body is no easy thing. Human birth is precious.
I: Oh, right. Every child is born from a womb.
Jushi: Don't think I'm making this up.
Jushi: There's science behind it. 
Jushi: The continuity of life is, after all, the transmission of information.
Jushi: Ordinary minds contain seeds of all kinds, wandering among the five realms of existence.
Jushi: What we call attaining sainthood is really the unification of one's mind.
Jushi: A sage's mind is one single, great seed. 
Jushi: Every rebirth of ours is determined by projecting karma and completing karma.
Jushi: Grow that seed of sagehood large enough, and naturally you master your own life.
Jushi: Perhaps even in the next one.
I: Oh... Well, I am grateful to have been born human in this life. 
[b]【I hear the call of [u]Ke Xiang[/u] and [u]Baochai[/u]】[/b]
Jushi: Indeed.
(She looks once more at the Buddha's Hand teapot and presses her lips together.)
<<jump JushiD2_FoShouConfirm>>
===


